ip`, the visitor's address\n- `url` and `request`, the page requested\n- `extension`, the file type\n- `machine.os`, the visitor's operating system\n- `geo.src` / `geo.dest`, where the request came from and went to\n- `agent` and `referer`\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-b95eb3ef Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-b95eb3ef-web-logs-overview-95e0f45d2ab1`). It has 7 panels:\n- **Total requests:** a single number counting all requests.\n- **Unique visitors:** a single number counting distinct `clientip` values.\n- **Total bytes served:** a single number adding up `bytes`.\n- **Requests over time:** a line chart of requests over time.\n- **Response codes:** a pie chart of how requests split across status codes.\n- **Top requested URLs:** a table of the 10 most requested URLs, with request count and bytes for each.\n- **Traffic by OS:** a bar chart of requests by visitor operating system.\n\nLink: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-b95eb3ef-web-logs-overview-95e0f45d2ab1\n\n**3. Alert rule: \"EFF-b95eb3ef 5xx spike\"** (id `7acbcf9c-b21e-48ee-bcf5-738407bff8a4`). It searches `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute. It fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive in the last 5 minutes, measured on `timestamp`. Because the response code is stored as text, the rule filters on the code's text value from \"500\" up to \"600\"; this works because every code has three digits.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so when it fires it only shows up in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone. If you want it to notify someone, you'll need to add a connector such as Slack or email.","ttft_ms":1916,"type":"result","duration_ms":30325,"uuid":"fe7b9953-4327-41c4-8147-8f408e0fb3b8","ttft_stream_ms":1654,"time_to_request_ms":82,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":20,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":9,"input_hooks":4,"tool_pool_refresh":2,"init_message":2,"engine_pickup":1,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autoco